"The stack is appropriate to the problem because the domain is defined by rules rather than by data volume. Marks computation, pass determination and credit accounting are rule-heavy and must be identical on every surface that reports them, which argues for a statically typed, modular server in which the calculators are shared components rather than replicated expressions in a client or a report writer. The relational model is not incidental: roll numbers are unique within an academic year rather than globally, a class context is the tuple of branch, semester, scheme and academic year, and one class teacher per class context is a uniqueness constraint rather than a convention. An object-relational mapper over a relational database keeps these invariants expressible and checkable, and the compound unique constraint on the mark idempotency key doubles as the index that the per-student result query traverses. The queue exists for a second reason that has nothing to do with scale: file parsing and report generation are operations whose failure must be isolated from the request that requested them, and a persisted job with a pollable status gives the operator a durable record of what was submitted and what became of it. The hosted services were chosen so that no local infrastructure is required to evaluate the system, while the local-disk fallback for storage and the null-tolerant cache mean that a missing external service degrades the system rather than disabling it.",
